# Untitled

## Rapid generation of specific antibodies by enhanced homologous recombination
Hidetaka Seo\(^{1,2}\), Mieko Masuoka\(^{1,2}\), Hiromu Murofushi\(^{3}\), Shunichi Takeda\(^{4}\), Takehiko Shibata\(^{5}\) & Kunihiro Ohta\(^{1}\)
In the chicken immune system, gene conversion, a type of homologous recombination, primarily contributes to diversification of the immunoglobulin gene\(^{1,2}\). Here, we report on the rapid generation of specific monoclonal antibodies using the chicken DT40 B-cell line undergoing gene conversion. We discovered that the gene conversion frequency at the immunoglobulin locus is increased by treating DT40 cells with a histone deacetylase inhibitor, trichostatin A (TSA)\(^{3}\), thereby generating diversity at the immunoglobulin locus in the majority of treated cells. This indicates that TSA treatment accelerates the autonomous diversification of surface IgMs on DT40 cells. We took advantage of this effect to select DT40 cells producing specific antibodies with antigen-conjugated magnetic beads. This autonomously diversifying library (ADLib) selection system enables the quick establishment (\(\sim\!1\) week from a diversifying library) of various clones producing monoclonal IgMs with enough specificity and affinity for immunological assays, and is applicable to various biotechnologies including national protein design.
Homologous recombination, together with mutagenesis and nonhomologous chromosomal rearrangements, is an important source of genetic variation required for various biological processes such as the
establishment of functional immune systems\(^{1,4}\). An enhancement of local chromatin DNA accessibility or transcriptional activity by modulation of chromatin structure through histone modification has been demonstrated to be a prerequisite for the initiation of various genetic rearrangements, including homologous recombination\(^{5-8}\).
The chicken immunoglobulin locus includes maternal and paternal homologous alleles\(^{9}\). In DT40 cells, it is known that one light chain allele is reconfigured by VJ rearrangement, whereas the other remains in the germline configuration\(^{10}\). In the VJ-rearranged allele, unidirectional gene conversion proceeds from the upstream 25 units of the pseudo V segments to the VJ-rearranged immunoglobulin light chain (IgLC) locus (active allele) to generate a preimmune repertoire\(^{11}\). The VJ-rearranged allele is actively transcribed, whereas the VJ-unrearranged allele shows no transcriptional or recombinational activity (inactive allele).
