Prolonged TSA treatment greatly enhanced the gene conversion at the active allele (Fig. 2a), as judged by the numbers of surface IgM (sIgM)-positive \(\mathrm{(IgM^{+})}\) cells in cultures of sIgM-negative \(\mathrm{(IgM^{-})}\) cells derived from a single clone generated by a frameshift mutation during autonomous gene conversion in DT40 cells\(^{11}\). In the absence of TSA, the ratio of \(\mathrm{IgM^{+}}\) cells is \(\sim\!1\%\) of the initial \(\mathrm{IgM^{-}}\) population. However, after 3–6 weeks of TSA treatment, \(60–90\%\) of the cells had converted to \(\mathrm{IgM^{+}}\). We found that this increase in the \(\mathrm{IgM^{+}}\) population depends upon TSA concentration and treatment time (Fig. 2b). The generation of \(\mathrm{IgM^{+}}\) cells slowed markedly when TSA was eliminated from the culture medium, suggesting that the effects are reversible (Fig. 2b). This enhancement of gene conversion
depending upon TSA concentration was further confirmed by fluctuation analysis after clonal expansion (Fig. 2c).
We conclude that TSA treatment markedly enhances gene conversion at the rearranged immunoglobulin allele in DT40 cells. However, we do not exclude the possibility that somatic hypermutation enhanced by TSA treatment partly contributes to the diversification of \(\mathrm{V_{L}}\), since a few mutation events were observed in the TSA-treated \(\mathrm{V_{L}}\) sequence (Fig. 2d,e).
These data suggest that TSA treatment accelerates antibody diversification and, thereby, autonomously generates a library of various cells presenting distinct sIgM on the cell surface. As the population of TSA-treated DT40 would contain immunoglobulin recombinants that might specifically recognize an antigen, we applied this TSA effect to an in vitro selection system of clones producing specific antibodies (Fig. 3).
Magnetic beads were covalently conjugated with purified human IgG (hIgG) as an antigen, and cells bound to the antigen beads were selected from \(\sim\!5\ \times\ 10^{7}\) DT40 cells treated with TSA for 6 weeks. The bound cells were diluted and all of them were cultured further in one 96-well plate (Fig. 3a); 76 wells in the plate contained viable cells. Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) revealed that within 1 week after selection, IgMs of culture supernatant from 14 of the 76 wells containing cells showed efficient binding to hIgG (data not shown). After clonal expansion, we analyzed the two subclones, HIGG8-1 and HIGG8-7, and found that the binding of their IgMs to hIgG is specific, whereas the culture supernatant of unselected cells showed no significant ELISA signal (Fig. 3b,c). We estimated the IgM concentration in the culture supernatant as
