One of the most successful in vitro antibody selection methods is the phage display system, in which single-chain phage-antibody hybrids can be selected from predesigned libraries16–18. The phage display system requires fixed libraries of high quality and large size, which require substantial time to prepare. Moreover, intact antibody molecules have to be reconstructed by additional recombinant DNA procedures. In the ADLib system, libraries are generated automatically during the passage process, and intact antibody molecules can be obtained directly. Alternatively, a recently described in vitro system, developed to mature antibody affinity by using a library derived from a constitutively hypermutating B-cell line could be used19,20. Cells that produced antibodies with higher affinities for antigens were efficiently isolated after several rounds of selection. The antibodies were in some cases polyreactive with unrelated proteins and specific antibodies were not obtained. Such polyreactive antibodies were obtained also in the ADLib system, but they could be eliminated by the screening step of ELISA using the target and unrelated antigens (Fig. 4a). Possibly, similar procedures might improve the efficiency of the mutation-based system.
In the ADLib system, clones producing a specific antibody can be selected even in a single round of screening, within 1 week if starting from a preexisting diversified library.
Such rapid generation of specific antibodies may be due to the nature of homologous recombination–based diversification, which can cause sequence segment exchanges that yield chimeras with greater sequence divergence than point mutations21. This is consistent with the widely accepted assumption that antibody specificity is initially acquired by the primary repertoire generated by \( \mathrm{V}(\mathrm{D})\mathrm{J} \) rearrangement or gene conversion, and that affinity maturation is eventually acquired by somatic hypermutation22,23. Combination of the ADLib system with the affinity maturation system will provide a complete system with which to generate monoclonal antibodies of high quality, mimicking the in vivo immune system. Such a system will offer tools for various biotechnology fields including rapid and high-throughput designs of monoclonal antibodies for medical and industrial purposes.
## METHODS
Cell culture. DT40 cells were propagated as previously described except for the use of Iscove's modified Dulbecco's medium. To examine antibody specificity with ELISA, we cultured cells in IgM-depleted medium prepared as follows. AIM-V serum-free medium (Invitrogen) was supplemented with \( 3{-}6\% \) IgM-depleted chicken serum. Chicken serum was depleted of IgM by ammonium sulfate precipitation (60% saturated) followed by dialysis of the supernatant against PBS.
ChIP assay. The acetylation of histone H4 was examined as described elsewhere12. Briefly, cell extracts prepared from crosslinked cells were incubated with anti-acetylated histone H4 (Upstate). The immune complexes were
collected by Dynabeads-ProteinA (Dynal) and washed thoroughly. DNA from whole cell extracts and their immunoprecipitates were purified and analyzed by semi-quantitative PCR using the Expand High Fidelity PCR system (Roche). The active \( \mathrm{V_{L}} \) allele was amplified using IGLC503- ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-CACACCTCAGGT ACTCGTTGCG-3') and -IGLC2995 ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-TCAGCGACTCACCTAGGACGG-3'); the inactive allele, using IGLC503- with -IGLC992 ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-GCTGCTCCTTGCACT GGCAG-3') and the ovalbumin promoter region using OV419- ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-CAGTGCT TTACAGAGGTCAGAA-3') with -OV923 ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-GTATCTACAACGTGCATCTGA GCC-3').
Northern blot analysis. The 3 kb EcoRI fragment containing the C region was used as a probe to detect IgG transcripts. The probe for the detection of the \( \upbeta \)-actin transcript was prepared by PCR amplification using a cloned chicken \( \upbeta \)-actin genomic EcoRI fragment as a template with primer pairs CBA4308- ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-GGTGAAGGTGTGAGCAGCAG-3') and -CBA4811 ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-GGCCA AGCAGCGCAGCACAG-3'). To obtain chicken AID cDNA for the detection of AID expression, a RACE library was prepared by Marathon-Ready cDNA kit (Clontech) from wild-type DT40 cells. From this RACE library, chicken AID cDNA was amplified by using primer pairs GdAID- \( 5^{\prime} \sim \) ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-CCCATGGACAGC CTCTTGATGAAGAGG-3') and \( \sim \) GdAID- \( 3^{\prime} \) ( \( 5^{\prime} \)-CCCTCTTCAAAGTCCCAG AGTTTAAAGG-3'). For northern blot analysis, \( \sim 20~\upmu\mathrm{g} \) of total RNA was denatured with formamide, separated in \( 1.5\% \) agarose gels containing formaldehyde, blotted onto a charged nylon membrane (PALL), hybridized with a \( \mathrm{P}^{32} \)-labeled probe and analyzed with BAS2500 (Fuji Film).
Analysis of gene conversion. Gene conversion frequency was measured as described24. The \( \mathrm{V_{L}} \) sequence was analyzed as described elsewhere with some modifications. Briefly, genomic DNA was amplified by PCR using Pyrobest (Takara-shuzo) polymerase \( 98\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), 2 min; 27 cycles of \( 98\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 30\ \mathrm{s} \); \( 57\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 30\ \mathrm{s} \); \( 72\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), 1min; and \( 72\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( 5\;\mathrm{min} \) ) and IGLC503-/-IGLC2995 as primers. After the addition of a terminal A by ExTaq (Takara) polymerase, the PCR products were cloned into the pCR2.1 TOPO vector and sequenced with an ABI 377 sequencer (Applied Biosystems) using M13 universal and reverse primers.
Antigen-beads selection. Before antigen-beads selection, sIgM was diversified by the treatment of DT40 cells with TSA \( (1.25{-}2.5\ \mathrm{ng/ml}) \) for 6–15 weeks. Selection was performed based on the method, previously described20 with some modifications. We mixed \( 5\,\times\,10^{7}–10^{8} \) TSA-treated DT40cells with \( 1{-}5\,\times\,10^{6} \) antigen-conjugated Dynabeads M280 Tosylactivated (Dynal) in \( 1\;\mathrm{ml} \) of selection buffer \( (\mathrm{PBS}/1\% \) BSA) and incubated at \( 4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 30\,\mathrm{\min} \). The magnetic beads were washed 3–5 times with selection buffer using an MPC-S (Dynal) magnet. Cells bound to the beads were added to \( 30\,\mathrm{\ml} \) of fresh medium, and transferred to one 96-well culture plate \( 300~\upmu\mathrm{l} \) in each well). After incubation for 1 week, the culture supernatants were analyzed by ELISA performed on immunoplates (NigelNunc) coated with antigen according to the manufacturer's instructions using horseradish peroxidase–conjugated goat anti-chicken IgM (Bethyl) as a secondary antibody. For the selection of antistreptavidin, antibody-producing cells, ELISA was performed on both streptavidin and ovalbumin, and a culture supernatant that reacted specifically with streptavidin was obtained. In the case of HIGG 8-1 and HIGG 8-7, to avoid cross-contamination of positive clones, we further performed limited dilution when required. Note that each of the data was obtained in a single round of selection.
Analysis of selected antibodies. The affinity and specificity of the selected sIgM producing cells were determined by FACS. Nearly \( 10^{6} \) cells were stained with \( 10\,\ \upmu\mathrm{g/ml} \) of FITC-conjugated antigens (hIgG, gIgG, rIgG (SIGMA), streptavidin (DAKO) and ovalbumin (Molecular Probe)) in selection buffer, washed with selection buffer and then analyzed. For the assay of secreted IgM, ELISA was performed on dilutions of culture supernatant obtained from \( 2{-}5\ \times\ 10^{5}/\mathrm{ml} \) cells incubated in IgM-depleted medium for \( 2\,\mathrm{~d~} \). The \( K_{\mathrm{d}} \) of the antibodies were determined by surface plasmon resonance by BiaCore2000 (BiaCore). Goat anti-chicken IgM antibody was covalently linked to CM5 sensor chips and IgM in the culture supernatant was immobilized. Antigens were diluted to various concentrations in HBS-EP buffer and
applied over the IgM-immobilized sensor chips. Data were statistically analyzed by Biaevaluation software.
